<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Active living hydration principles">
  <div><strong>Water often works</strong><span>For many short or moderate activities, water and meals are enough.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Heat and duration matter</strong><span>Longer, harder or hotter sessions make carbohydrate-electrolyte drinks more useful.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Match the drink to the session</strong><span>Needs vary with sweat loss, pace, body size and environment.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Keep it simple</p>
    <h2>Hydration should support the activity, not become the activity</h2>
    <p>A walk, yoga class or short gym session does not automatically require a sports drink. Water, normal meals and easy access to fluid remain a strong default.</p>
    <p>Long hikes, hot rides and extended tournaments can create more meaningful sweat losses. In those situations, carrying enough safe fluid and using a carbohydrate-electrolyte drink can make more sense.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Choose by session</p>
  <h2>A simple activity spectrum</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Light movement</h3><p>Walking, stretching and errands usually fit normal water and meal habits.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Short workout</h3><p>Water is generally a sensible choice unless heat or sweat loss is unusually high.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Long recreation</h3><p>Long hikes, rides, tournaments and active days can make electrolytes and carbohydrate more useful.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Performance training</h3><p>When competition and repeatability matter, move to a more individualized plan.</p><a href="/pages/performance-hydration">Performance Hydration →</a></article>
  </div>
</section>

